<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-11-case-study" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Case Study: A Community Kitchen Bakery Tracker</title>

  <introduction>
    <sidebyside widths="58% 38%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <p>
        Commercial baking equipment is expensive. To lower the barrier for aspiring food
        entrepreneurs, many communities organize shared-use commissary kitchens and baker
        cooperatives. In these community kitchens, multiple independent bakers share high-end
        commercial equipment—such as 60-quart spiral mixers and multi-deck stone baking
        ovens—sharing utility expenses and maintenance overhead. Imagine you have been hired
        by a non-profit culinary incubator to write a software tool that tracks dough batch
        production, manages shared oven runtime, enforces baking safety invariants, and calculates
        accurate production costs per loaf for member bakers.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="figure-community-bakery">
        <caption>Artisan bakers collaborating in a shared community commercial kitchen. (Image generated with AI).</caption>
        <image source="figures/community_bakery_header.png">
          <shortdescription>Artisan bakers working side-by-side preparing sourdough loaves in a shared commercial bakery kitchen.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
    <p>
      This case study integrates all the core object-oriented principles introduced in this chapter:
      state encapsulation, constructors with validation, class invariants, object composition,
      message passing, and state-transition enforcement verified via unit tests.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-case-study-domain-model">
    <title>Domain Modeling and CRC Cards</title>
    <p>
      Before writing Java code, object-oriented designers identify the key domain entities,
      their specific responsibilities, and how they collaborate. A proven design tool for this
      stage is the <term>Class-Responsibility-Collaborator (CRC) card</term><fn>Kent Beck and Ward
      Cunningham, <q>A Laboratory For Teaching Object-Oriented Thinking,</q> <pubtitle>OOPSLA '89
      Conference Proceedings</pubtitle>, ACM, 1989.</fn>, which uses a simple index card layout
      to record a class's name, responsibilities, and collaborators.
    </p>
    <p>
      For our community bakery tracker, we identify two primary domain classes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>Oven</c>: Models a shared piece of physical kitchen equipment. Its responsibilities
          include tracking its capacity, hourly energy rate, and cumulative runtime across all
          bakers who use it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>RecipeBatch</c>: Models a specific batch of dough prepared by an entrepreneur.
          Its responsibilities include tracking recipe name, loaf yield, ingredient cost, bake
          time, and whether the batch has been baked. It collaborates with an <c>Oven</c> to
          complete baking and compute total cost per loaf.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto" valign="top">
      <figure xml:id="figure-6-22">
        <caption>Figure 6-22: CRC card for the <c>RecipeBatch</c> class.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_22.png">
          <shortdescription>CRC card for RecipeBatch showing responsibilities such as tracking recipe name, ingredients, and bake state, collaborating with Oven.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="figure-6-23">
        <caption>Figure 6-23: CRC card for the <c>Oven</c> class.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_23.png">
          <shortdescription>CRC card for Oven showing responsibilities such as recording runtime and calculating cumulative operating costs.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-case-study-class-specifications">
    <title>UML Class Specifications</title>
    <p>
      Translating the CRC cards into formal UML class specifications clarifies each class's
      internal state representation and public method signatures. Following standard UML
      conventions, private members are marked with a minus sign (<c>-</c>) and public members
      with a plus sign (<c>+</c>).
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-class-diagram-oven">
      <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>Oven</c></title>
      <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell halign="center"><strong>Oven</strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>- name : String</p>
            <p>- maxCapacity : int</p>
            <p>- hourlyOperatingCost : double</p>
            <p>- totalHoursRun : double</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>+ Oven(name : String, maxCapacity : int, hourlyOperatingCost : double)</p>
            <p>+ getName() : String</p>
            <p>+ getMaxCapacity() : int</p>
            <p>+ getHourlyOperatingCost() : double</p>
            <p>+ getTotalHoursRun() : double</p>
            <p>+ recordRunTime(hours : double) : void</p>
            <p>+ calculateCumulativeEnergyCost() : double</p>
            <p>+ toString() : String</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

<table xml:id="table-class-diagram-recipe-batch">
      <title>UML Class Diagram for <c>RecipeBatch</c></title>
      <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="100%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell halign="center"><strong>RecipeBatch</strong></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>- recipeName : String</p>
            <p>- yieldLoaves : int</p>
            <p>- ingredientCost : double</p>
            <p>- bakeTimeHours : double</p>
            <p>- baked : boolean</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <p>+ RecipeBatch(recipeName : String, yieldLoaves : int, ingredientCost : double, bakeTimeHours : double)</p>
            <p>+ getRecipeName() : String</p>
            <p>+ getYieldLoaves() : int</p>
            <p>+ getIngredientCost() : double</p>
            <p>+ getBakeTimeHours() : double</p>
            <p>+ isBaked() : boolean</p>
            <p>+ bake(oven : Oven) : void</p>
            <p>+ calculateTotalCost(oven : Oven) : double</p>
            <p>+ calculateCostPerLoaf(oven : Oven) : double</p>
            <p>+ toString() : String</p>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-case-study-state-machine">
    <title>State-Transition Modeling</title>
    <p>
      An essential responsibility of <c>RecipeBatch</c> is lifecycle state management. Once a batch
      of dough has been baked in an oven, it cannot be unbaked, nor should a baker accidentally
      send the same batch through the baking cycle a second time.
    </p>
    <p>
      We model this behavior using a UML state-transition diagram (<xref ref="figure-6-24"/>).
      When constructed, a <c>RecipeBatch</c> enters the <c>Unbaked</c> state with <c>baked == false</c>.
      Invoking <c>bake(oven)</c> transitions the batch to the <c>Baked</c> state, recording the bake
      time on the oven. If <c>bake(oven)</c> is invoked on an already baked batch, the method rejects
      the transition and throws an <c>AssertionError</c>, maintaining the batch's integrity.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="figure-6-24">
      <caption>Figure 6-24: State-transition diagram for <c>RecipeBatch</c>.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_24.png" width="90%">
        <shortdescription>UML State-Transition diagram showing transition from Unbaked to Baked on bake(), and a solid self-loop on Baked indicating AssertionError on duplicate bake attempts.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-case-study-step1-stubs">
    <title>Step 1: Outlines and Class Invariants</title>
    <p>
      We begin implementation by defining class outlines that document the class invariants.
      Every field is marked <c>private</c>, ensuring encapsulation.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-oven-outline">
      <caption>Outline and invariants for <c>Oven.java</c>.</caption>
      <program language="java">
        <input>
<![CDATA[
public class Oven {
    // Class Invariants:
    // 1. name != null && !name.trim().isEmpty()
    // 2. maxCapacity > 0
    // 3. hourlyOperatingCost >= 0.0
    // 4. totalHoursRun >= 0.0

    private String name;
    private int maxCapacity;
    private double hourlyOperatingCost;
    private double totalHoursRun;

    public Oven(String name, int maxCapacity, double hourlyOperatingCost) {
        // Constructor validation will enforce invariants
    }

    public void recordRunTime(double hours) {
        // Must validate hours > 0.0 before updating totalHoursRun
    }

    public double calculateCumulativeEnergyCost() {
        return 0.0; // Stub
    }
}
]]>
        </input>
      </program>
    </listing>

